From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Implement Windows.ApplicationModel.Core.CoreApplication

Real Microsoft software built on the Windows app model activates
Windows.ApplicationModel.Core.CoreApplication at start-up, before it does any
work. winget (WindowsPackageManager.dll) does this while bringing up its
telemetry: it reads CoreApplication.Properties, a process-wide property bag, to
stash and find process singletons. Upstream Wine ships the ICoreApplication
type but registers no runtime class for it -- the registration in
windows.applicationmodel.core.idl is guarded out of the one DLL that has the
`register' pragma -- so RoGetActivationFactory returned REGDB_E_CLASSNOTREG.
winget wrapped that in a throw, the throw unwound through a noexcept boundary,
and the process terminated before printing anything: `winget --version' died
silently.

This registers and implements CoreApplication in windows.applicationmodel.dll:

 - windows.applicationmodel.core.idl: the CoreApplication runtime class carried
   full activation attributes but sat inside `#ifndef _WINDOWS_APPLICATIONMODEL',
   and classes.idl (the only `register' consumer) defines that macro -- so the
   registration was emitted nowhere. Drop the guard so the class registers to
   this DLL.

 - core_application.c: an ICoreApplication factory. Id returns empty (an
   unpackaged process has no app-model identity); the Suspending/Resuming events
   accept subscriptions that are never raised; Properties returns a single
   process-wide property set.

 - property_set.c: a small functional IPropertySet -- an ordinal-keyed map of
   IInspectable values, with the IObservableMap and IIterable faces present.
   Wine had none (wintypes registers ValueSet but activates it as a stub), and
   CoreApplication.Properties needs a real one.

With this and the KnownFolderPath fix (previous patch), real winget-cli starts
under Wine: `winget --version' and `winget --help' run.

